(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["anatomy-exam-3-male-reproductive-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Male Reproductive System — Anatomy Exam 3 (Set 2)","category":"Anatomy","questions":[{"q":"During development, the testes first form inside the abdominal cavity near which organs?","choices":["The kidneys","The liver","The spleen","The adrenal glands"],"answer":0,"exp":"The testes develop inside the abdominal cavity near the kidneys before descending toward the scrotum. — Slide 9 (Descent of the Testes)"},{"q":"Which structure holds the testes in place and, by not elongating during body growth, pulls them inferiorly toward the scrotum?","choices":["The spermatic cord bundle","The gubernaculum testis","The tunica albuginea","The pampiniform plexus"],"answer":1,"exp":"The gubernaculum testis anchors the testes; as the body grows it fails to elongate, drawing the testes downward through the inguinal canals. — Slide 9 (Descent of the Testes)"},{"q":"As the testes descend, they pass through which passage that connects the scrotal chambers to the peritoneal cavity?","choices":["The inguinal canals","The obturator foramen","The greater sciatic foramen","The femoral canal"],"answer":0,"exp":"The testes descend through the inguinal canals; the ductus deferens, vessels, lymphatics, and nerves remain bundled with them in the spermatic cords. — Slides 9, 14 (Descent / Spermatic cords)"},{"q":"The requirement that sperm develop at ~2°F below core body temperature explains why:","choices":["The testes remain within the abdominal cavity","The prostate gland enlarges progressively with age","The testes descend to the external scrotum","Sperm mature within the seminal vesicles"],"answer":2,"exp":"Sperm development needs ~96.6°F (about 2°F cooler than body temperature), so the testes reside in the external scrotum. — Slide 10 (Descent of the Testes)"},{"q":"Which superficial smooth muscle of the scrotum wrinkles the scrotal skin when it contracts?","choices":["Cremaster muscle","Bulbospongiosus","Dartos muscle","Ischiocavernosus"],"answer":2,"exp":"The dartos is a superficial smooth muscle whose contraction wrinkles the scrotal surface; the cremaster elevates the testes. — Slide 18 (Muscles of the scrotum)"},{"q":"Contraction of the cremaster muscle produces which effect?","choices":["Tensing the scrotal sac and pulling the testes closer to the body","Wrinkling of the scrotal skin via the dartos muscle","Ejaculation of semen through the urethral orifice","Erection of the penis via parasympathetic vasodilation"],"answer":0,"exp":"The cremaster tenses the scrotal sac and elevates the testes toward the body to maintain sperm-appropriate temperature. — Slide 19 (Cremaster muscle)"},{"q":"During ejaculation, forceful contractions of which two muscles push semen toward the external urethral orifice?","choices":["Dartos and cremaster","Ischiocavernosus and bulbospongiosus","Puborectalis and pubococcygeus","Rectus abdominis and transversus abdominis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Ejaculation involves powerful contractions of the ischiocavernosus and bulbospongiosus muscles. — Slide 67 (Semen release and ejaculation)"},{"q":"The scrotum is highly vascularized by the internal pudendal artery, the external pudendal artery, and the:","choices":["Middle rectal artery","Superior mesenteric artery","Obturator artery","Inferior epigastric artery"],"answer":3,"exp":"Scrotal supply comes from the internal pudendal (from internal iliac), external pudendal (from femoral), and inferior epigastric (from external iliac) arteries. — Slide 21 (Scrotum vascularization)"},{"q":"The internal pudendal artery supplying pelvic/reproductive structures is a branch of which vessel?","choices":["Femoral artery","Internal iliac artery","External iliac artery","Abdominal aorta directly"],"answer":1,"exp":"The internal pudendal artery arises from the internal iliac artery. — Slide 21 (Scrotum vascularization)"},{"q":"The external pudendal artery is a branch of which vessel?","choices":["Splenic artery","Internal iliac artery","Inferior mesenteric artery","Femoral artery"],"answer":3,"exp":"The external pudendal artery is a branch of the femoral artery. — Slide 21 (Scrotum vascularization)"},{"q":"Within the spermatic cord, the venous drainage of the testis forms a network called the:","choices":["Vesical venous plexus","Prostatic venous plexus","Pampiniform plexus","Vertebral venous plexus"],"answer":2,"exp":"The pampiniform plexus of the testicular vein is a component of the spermatic cord and aids in cooling arterial blood to the testis. — Slide 14 (The Spermatic Cords)"},{"q":"The deep artery of the penis, responsible for engorging erectile tissue, lies in the center of which structure?","choices":["The corpus spongiosum","The bulb of the penis","Each corpus cavernosum","The glans penis tissue"],"answer":2,"exp":"The deep artery of the penis runs in the center of each corpus cavernosum. — Slide 62 (The Penis)"},{"q":"The scrotum receives sensory and motor nerves from the hypogastric plexus, the pudendal nerves, the genitofemoral nerves, and the:","choices":["Femoral nerves","Phrenic nerves","Obturator nerves","Ilioinguinal nerves"],"answer":3,"exp":"Scrotal innervation includes the hypogastric plexus, ilioinguinal, genitofemoral, and pudendal nerves. — Slide 22 (Scrotum innervation)"},{"q":"Which two nerves are named as components running with the spermatic cord?","choices":["Vagus and phrenic nerves of the thorax","Pudendal and obturator nerves of the pelvis","Femoral and sciatic nerves of the lower limb","Ilioinguinal and genitofemoral nerves"],"answer":3,"exp":"The spermatic cord contains the ilioinguinal and genitofemoral nerves along with the ductus deferens and vessels. — Slide 14 (The Spermatic Cords)"},{"q":"Erection of the penis is mediated by which division of the autonomic nervous system?","choices":["Sympathetic nerves","Parasympathetic nerves","Somatic motor nerves only","The enteric nervous system"],"answer":1,"exp":"Parasympathetic activation relaxes arterial smooth muscle, dilating vessels and engorging erectile tissue to produce an erection ('point' = parasympathetic). — Slide 66 (Erection of the penis)"},{"q":"Emission and ejaculation of semen are primarily driven by which autonomic activity?","choices":["Parasympathetic activity","Purely voluntary control","Vagal activity","Sympathetic activity"],"answer":3,"exp":"Emission and ejaculation are driven by sympathetic activity ('shoot' = sympathetic), mixing sperm with glandular secretions and expelling semen. — Slide 67 (Semen release and ejaculation)"},{"q":"Which fibrous connective-tissue layer directly lines the testis and sends septa that converge at the mediastinum of the testis?","choices":["Tunica albuginea","Tunica vaginalis","Dartos fascia","Cremasteric fascia"],"answer":0,"exp":"The tunica albuginea lines the testis and forms septa converging toward the mediastinum, which supports the ducts leading to the epididymis. — Slide 23 (Structure of the Testes)"},{"q":"The serous membrane that surrounds the tunica albuginea and reduces friction within each scrotal cavity is the:","choices":["Glisson's capsule","Dartos muscle","Buck's fascia","Tunica vaginalis"],"answer":3,"exp":"The tunica vaginalis is a serous membrane lining each scrotal cavity, continuous with the epididymal covering, reducing friction. — Slides 16, 23 (Tunica vaginalis)"},{"q":"Within each testicular lobule, sperm are produced in tightly coiled tubules called:","choices":["Efferent ductules","Collecting ducts","Seminiferous tubules","Straight tubules"],"answer":2,"exp":"Each lobule contains coiled seminiferous tubules (~80 cm each) where spermatogenesis occurs. — Slide 25 (Histology of the Testes)"},{"q":"Which sequence correctly traces sperm through the intratesticular ducts?","choices":["Seminiferous tubules → straight tubules → rete testis → efferent ductules → epididymis","Rete testis → seminiferous tubules → epididymis → efferent ductules","Efferent ductules → rete testis → seminiferous tubules → epididymis","Epididymis → rete testis → straight tubules → seminiferous tubules"],"answer":0,"exp":"Sperm pass from seminiferous tubules to straight tubules, then the rete testis (in the mediastinum), then efferent ductules to the epididymis. — Slide 25 (Histology of the Testes)"},{"q":"The interstitial endocrine cells (Leydig cells) located between the seminiferous tubules produce:","choices":["Testosterone","Inhibin","Androgen-binding protein","Fructose"],"answer":0,"exp":"Leydig (interstitial endocrine) cells produce and release testosterone. — Slide 27 (Histology of the Testes)"},{"q":"The nurse cells (Sertoli cells) of the seminiferous tubules maintain the blood–testis barrier and secrete inhibin plus:","choices":["Testosterone-binding globulin","Prostaglandins from seminal fluid","Fructose for sperm metabolism","Androgen-binding protein (ABP)"],"answer":3,"exp":"Sertoli (nurse) cells secrete inhibin (controls sperm formation rate) and androgen-binding protein, and maintain the blood–testis barrier to prevent autoimmune destruction of sperm. — Slide 36 (Nurse Cells)"},{"q":"The epididymis is divided into three parts; which part receives spermatozoa from the efferent ductules?","choices":["The tail","The body","The head","The isthmus"],"answer":2,"exp":"The head of the epididymis receives spermatozoa via the efferent ducts; the tail uncoils to become the ductus deferens. — Slide 42 (The Epididymis)"},{"q":"Which three glands are the accessory glands of the male reproductive system?","choices":["Parotid, submandibular, and sublingual glands","Seminal glands, prostate, and bulbo-urethral glands","Adrenal, thyroid, and parathyroid glands","Cowper's, Bartholin's, and Skene's glands"],"answer":1,"exp":"The accessory glands are the seminal glands (vesicles), the prostate, and the bulbo-urethral (Cowper's) glands. — Slide 53 (The Accessory Glands)"},{"q":"The seminal glands produce approximately what percentage of the semen volume, with fluid rich in fructose, prostaglandins, and clotting factors?","choices":["5 percent","20–30 percent","60 percent","95 percent"],"answer":2,"exp":"The seminal glands produce ~60% of semen volume; fructose provides energy for sperm motility. — Slide 55 (The Seminal Glands)"},{"q":"The prostate secretes a weakly acidic fluid comprising about 20–30% of semen volume that contains enzymes and an antibiotic substance called:","choices":["Seminalplasmin","Hyaluronidase","Inhibin","Androgen-binding protein"],"answer":0,"exp":"Prostatic fluid (20–30% of semen) is weakly acidic and contains seminalplasmin, an antibiotic thought to prevent UTIs in males. — Slide 56 (The Prostate)"},{"q":"The bulbo-urethral (Cowper's) glands secrete a thick alkaline mucus that neutralizes urinary acids and:","choices":["Provides energy via fructose metabolism","Triggers erection through parasympathetic input","Provides lubrication for the tip of the penis","Coagulates semen within the vaginal canal"],"answer":2,"exp":"Bulbo-urethral secretions (~5% of semen) are alkaline mucus that neutralize residual urinary acid and lubricate the penile tip. — Slide 57 (Bulbo-urethral Glands)"},{"q":"The penis contains three columns of erectile tissue: two posterior corpora cavernosa and one anterior:","choices":["Corpus callosum","Corpus luteum tissue","Corpus spongiosum","Bulbospongiosus tissue"],"answer":2,"exp":"The penis has two dorsal/posterior corpora cavernosa and one ventral/anterior corpus spongiosum surrounding the spongy urethra. — Slide 61 (The Penis)"},{"q":"The corpus spongiosum surrounds the spongy urethra and its thickened proximal end forms the:","choices":["Crus of the penis","Prepuce","Glans penis","Bulb of the penis"],"answer":3,"exp":"The corpus spongiosum's proximal enlargement forms the bulb of the penis; each corpus cavernosum extends posteriorly as a crus bound to the ischial ramus. — Slide 62 (The Penis)"},{"q":"The head of a spermatozoon contains chromosomes and an enzyme-filled cap essential for fertilization called the:","choices":["Acrosome","Flagellum","Centriole","Mitochondrial spiral"],"answer":0,"exp":"The sperm head contains the acrosome (with hyaluronidase) needed to penetrate the egg; the midpiece holds mitochondria and the tail is the flagellum. — Slide 38 (Anatomy of a Sperm)"},{"q":"Which sequence correctly traces the pathway of sperm from production to the ejaculatory duct?","choices":["Ductus deferens → epididymis → seminiferous tubules → ejaculatory duct","Seminiferous tubules → epididymis → ductus deferens → ejaculatory duct","Epididymis → seminiferous tubules → ejaculatory duct → ductus deferens","Ejaculatory duct → ductus deferens → epididymis → seminiferous tubules"],"answer":1,"exp":"Sperm form in the seminiferous tubules, mature/store in the epididymis, travel up the ductus deferens, and enter the ejaculatory duct. — Slides 25, 42, 47–48 (sperm pathway)"},{"q":"Before entering the prostate, the ductus deferens expands into the ampulla, which joins the excretory duct of the seminal gland to form the:","choices":["Ejaculatory duct","Prostatic urethra","Membranous urethra","Efferent ductule"],"answer":0,"exp":"The ampulla of the ductus deferens joins the seminal gland's excretory duct to form the ejaculatory duct, which empties into the prostatic urethra. — Slide 48 (The Ductus Deferens)"},{"q":"The ejaculatory duct empties into which part of the urethra?","choices":["External urethral orifice","Membranous urethra","Spongy urethra","Prostatic urethra"],"answer":3,"exp":"The ejaculatory duct empties into the prostatic urethra within the prostate gland. — Slide 48 (The Ductus Deferens)"},{"q":"Mature sperm stored in the epididymis are not yet able to fertilize an egg until they undergo which process outside the epididymis?","choices":["Spermiogenesis","Spermatogenesis","Meiosis I","Capacitation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Capacitation occurs outside the epididymis: sperm become motile when mixed with seminal-gland secretions and gain fertilizing ability in the female tract. — Slides 44–45 (The Epididymis)"},{"q":"The ductus deferens ascends from the tail of the epididymis into the abdomen through which passage?","choices":["The femoral canal","The inguinal canal","The obturator canal","The pudendal canal"],"answer":1,"exp":"The ductus (vas) deferens ascends through the inguinal canal, then curves around the bladder and ureter toward the prostate. — Slides 47–48 (The Ductus Deferens)"},{"q":"Approximately what percentage of semen volume comes from the prostate during ejaculation?","choices":["30 percent","5 percent","60 percent","90 percent"],"answer":0,"exp":"Semen composition is ~60% seminal vesicles, ~30% prostate, ~5% bulbo-urethral glands, and ~5% epididymis. — Slide 59 (Semen)"},{"q":"The male urethra is divided into three regions in which correct proximal-to-distal order?","choices":["Prostatic → membranous → spongy (penile)","Spongy → membranous → prostatic","Membranous → prostatic → spongy","Prostatic → spongy → membranous"],"answer":0,"exp":"From bladder to tip: prostatic urethra (through the prostate), membranous urethra (through the pelvic floor/UG diaphragm), then spongy/penile urethra. — Slide 51 (The Urethra)"},{"q":"The prostatic urethra is the portion of the urethra that:","choices":["Passes through the penis","Extends from the bladder through the prostate gland","Passes through the muscular wall of the pelvic cavity","Ends at the external urethral orifice"],"answer":1,"exp":"The prostatic urethra extends from the urinary bladder through the prostate gland. — Slide 51 (The Urethra)"},{"q":"The membranous urethra is characterized by:","choices":["Passing through the substance of the prostate gland","Passing through the muscular wall of the pelvic cavity (pelvic floor)","Passing through the entire length of the penile shaft","Being the short terminal segment opening at the glans"],"answer":1,"exp":"The membranous urethra passes through the muscular wall/floor of the pelvic cavity between the prostatic and spongy portions. — Slide 51 (The Urethra)"},{"q":"The spongy (penile) urethra runs within which erectile column and ends at the external urethral orifice?","choices":["Corpus cavernosum","Tunica albuginea","Bulb of the penis crus","Corpus spongiosum"],"answer":3,"exp":"The spongy urethra passes through the corpus spongiosum along the length of the penis to the external urethral orifice on the glans. — Slides 51, 62 (spongy urethra / corpus spongiosum)"},{"q":"The bulbo-urethral glands empty their secretions into which part of the urethra, just distal to the membranous urethra?","choices":["Prostatic urethra","Directly into the bladder","Spongy urethra","The ejaculatory duct"],"answer":2,"exp":"Bulbo-urethral gland contents empty into the spongy urethra immediately distal to the body wall (end of the membranous urethra). — Slide 57 (Bulbo-urethral Glands)"},{"q":"The spermatic cords, which remain bundled with the descended testes, contain the ductus deferens, nerves, lymphatics, and:","choices":["The prostate gland, situated inferior to the bladder neck","Blood vessels (deferential and testicular arteries, pampiniform plexus)","The seminal vesicles, lying posterior to the bladder base","The ejaculatory duct, formed within the prostate gland"],"answer":1,"exp":"The spermatic cord contains the ductus deferens, deferential and testicular arteries, pampiniform plexus, lymphatics, and nerves. — Slide 14 (The Spermatic Cords)"},{"q":"Testicle development itself requires a temperature of approximately:","choices":["96.6°F","98.6°F","94.0°F","101.0°F"],"answer":1,"exp":"Testicle development requires ~98.6°F (hence initial abdominal development), whereas sperm development requires the cooler ~96.6°F of the scrotum. — Slide 10 (Descent of the Testes)"},{"q":"Spermatogonia, the stem cells that give rise to sperm, are formed during embryonic development but remain dormant until:","choices":["Birth","The male climacteric","Middle age","Puberty"],"answer":3,"exp":"Spermatogonia form during embryonic development but stay dormant until puberty, when they begin continuous division. — Slide 30 (Spermatogenesis and Meiosis)"},{"q":"The raphe of the scrotum, which separates the two scrotal cavities, appears as thickened tissue extending from the anus across the scrotum to the:","choices":["Perineal body region only","The inguinal ligament fold","The pubic symphysis joint","Anterior surface of the penis"],"answer":3,"exp":"The scrotal raphe marks the midline septum and extends from the anus across the scrotum onto the anterior surface of the penis. — Slide 16 (Scrotum and Position of the Testes)"},{"q":"Which paired muscles contract during ejaculation to help expel semen — the same muscles associated with the penile bulb and crura?","choices":["Bulbospongiosus and ischiocavernosus","Dartos and cremaster muscles","Levator ani and coccygeus muscles","Rectus abdominis and pyramidalis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Bulbospongiosus (over the bulb/corpus spongiosum) and ischiocavernosus (over the crura) contract forcefully during ejaculation. — Slide 67 (ejaculation muscles)"},{"q":"The inferior epigastric artery, contributing to scrotal supply, is a branch of which vessel?","choices":["Internal iliac artery","External iliac artery","Femoral artery","Abdominal aorta"],"answer":1,"exp":"The inferior epigastric artery is a branch of the external iliac artery. — Slide 21 (Scrotum vascularization)"},{"q":"The pampiniform plexus surrounding the testicular artery functions primarily to:","choices":["Increase testicular temperature via arterial warming","Produce testosterone directly within Leydig cell clusters","Store mature sperm within the tail of the epididymis","Cool arterial blood entering the testis via a countercurrent mechanism"],"answer":3,"exp":"The pampiniform plexus wraps the testicular artery, cooling incoming arterial blood to help maintain the lower temperature sperm require. — Slides 14–15 (Spermatic cord / pampiniform plexus)"},{"q":"Which artery specifically accompanies the ductus deferens within the spermatic cord?","choices":["Deferential artery","Internal pudendal artery","Obturator artery","Inferior vesical artery"],"answer":0,"exp":"The deferential artery runs with the ductus deferens in the spermatic cord alongside the testicular artery. — Slides 14–15 (Spermatic cord vessels)"},{"q":"The genitofemoral nerve, which supplies the scrotum and cremaster, is identified as a component of which structure?","choices":["The pudendal canal","The spermatic cord","The obturator canal","The femoral sheath"],"answer":1,"exp":"The genitofemoral nerve travels with the spermatic cord and contributes motor/sensory supply to the scrotum and cremaster. — Slides 14–15, 22 (spermatic cord / scrotal innervation)"},{"q":"During emission, sperm are mixed with glandular secretions by peristaltic contraction of smooth muscle in the ductus deferens, seminal glands, prostate, and bulbo-urethral glands under which control?","choices":["Parasympathetic control","Somatic voluntary control","Sympathetic control","Vagal control"],"answer":2,"exp":"Emission is a sympathetic event; peristalsis of the ducts and glands moves sperm and secretions into the urethra prior to ejaculation. — Slide 67 (Semen release and ejaculation)"},{"q":"A single primary spermatocyte undergoing meiosis ultimately gives rise to how many sperm cells?","choices":["Two","One","Four","Eight"],"answer":2,"exp":"A primary spermatocyte yields two secondary spermatocytes (meiosis I), each producing two spermatids (meiosis II), for a total of four haploid sperm. — Slide 32 (Spermatogenesis and Meiosis)"},{"q":"The physical maturation of spermatids into sperm cells, occurring while embedded in Sertoli (nurse) cells, is called:","choices":["Spermatogenesis","Spermiogenesis","Capacitation","Meiosis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Spermiogenesis is the maturation of spermatids into sperm; capacitation (fertilizing ability) occurs later, outside the epididymis. — Slide 35 (Spermiogenesis)"},{"q":"The middle piece (with the neck) of a spermatozoon contains mitochondria and:","choices":["The acrosome","Chromosomes","Centrioles","The flagellum tip"],"answer":2,"exp":"The midpiece/neck contains mitochondria (energy) and centrioles; the head holds chromosomes and the acrosome, and the tail is the flagellum. — Slide 38 (Anatomy of a Sperm)"},{"q":"The blood–testis barrier, maintained by nurse (Sertoli) cells, functions to:","choices":["Produce testosterone directly within the seminiferous tubules","Store mature sperm prior to release into the ductus deferens","Isolate developing sperm from the circulation to prevent autoimmune destruction","Secrete prostatic fluid directly into the ejaculatory pathway"],"answer":2,"exp":"Nurse cells form the blood–testis barrier, isolating seminiferous tubules from the circulation so the immune system does not attack developing sperm. — Slide 36 (Nurse Cells)"},{"q":"The prepuce (foreskin) attaches to the neck of the glans; preputial glands secrete a waxy material called:","choices":["Smegma","Colloid","Seminalplasmin","Meconium"],"answer":0,"exp":"Preputial glands secrete waxy smegma, which can encourage bacterial growth; circumcision removes the prepuce to avoid this. — Slide 63 (The Penis)"},{"q":"The efferent ductules connect which two structures?","choices":["The epididymis and the prostatic urethra","The seminiferous tubules and the rete testis","The ductus deferens and the ejaculatory duct","The rete testis and the epididymis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Efferent ductules lead from the rete testis (in the mediastinum) to the head of the epididymis. — Slide 25 (Histology of the Testes)"},{"q":"After the tail of the epididymis, the duct that transports sperm toward the abdomen is called the:","choices":["Efferent ductule","Ejaculatory duct","Ductus (vas) deferens","Straight tubule"],"answer":2,"exp":"The ductus deferens (vas deferens) begins at the tail of the epididymis and ascends through the inguinal canal. — Slide 47 (The Ductus Deferens)"},{"q":"Approximately how much semen is released per ejaculation, per the lecture?","choices":["2 to 5 mL","0.5 to 1 mL","10 to 15 mL","20 to 30 mL"],"answer":0,"exp":"Each ejaculation releases about 2–5 mL of semen, with a normal sperm count of 20–100 million per mL. — Slide 59 (Semen)"},{"q":"The male reproductive tract, involved in maturation, storage, and transport of sperm, consists of the epididymis, the ductus deferens, and the:","choices":["Seminal gland","Urethra","Prostate","Bulbo-urethral gland"],"answer":1,"exp":"The male reproductive tract proper is the epididymis, ductus deferens, and urethra; the seminal glands, prostate, and bulbo-urethral glands are accessory glands. — Slide 41 (The Male Reproductive Tract)"}]};
